==Old English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{recons|skīnanan|lang=gem-pro}}, from an Indo-European root ''*skai-, *ski-''. Cognate with Old High German {{term|skinan|skīnan|lang=goh}} (German {{term|scheinen|lang=de}}), Old Saxon {{term|skinan|skīnan|lang=osx}} (Dutch {{term|schijnen|lang=nl}}), Old Norse {{term|skína|lang=non}} (Swedish {{term|skina|lang=sv}}), Gothic {{term|𐍃𐌺𐌴𐌹𐌽𐌰𐌽|tr=skeinan|lang=got}}. The Indo-European root is also the source of Ancient Greek {{term|σκιά|tr=skiá|lang=grc}}, Old Slavic ''sěn’'' (Russian {{term|сень|tr=sen’}}).

